// A congruence closure that survives RETRACTION — no union-find.
//
// Classical e-graphs cannot un-merge. `union(a, b)` is destructive, and path
// compression discards exactly the provenance you would need to undo it, so
// deleting an equation means rebuilding from scratch. Every e-graph library
// (egg, egglog, eqlog, Cranelift's ægraphs) is insert-only for this reason;
// Souffle's `eqrel` states the assumption outright ("Datalog relations are
// growing monotonically, and hence no deletion operations are required").
//
// This factors the e-graph the way `avg` factors into `sum` + `count`. An
// average cannot be maintained incrementally because two averages do not
// combine; sum and count each do, and the average is recovered at the end.
// A union-find cannot be retracted because merging forgets why; so replace it
// with pieces that each remain a monotone VIEW over the asserted facts:
//
//   term      the term universe
//   eq_edge   asserted equations, plus those congruence derives (symmetric)
//   leader    term -> class representative — a merge(min) LATTICE FOLD over
//             the equation graph, i.e. label propagation, not a union-find
//   cnode     each e-node with its children canonicalized (a view)
//   form_rep  the hash-cons: representative term per canonical form
//
// Nothing is mutated. The class structure is a function of the asserted
// equations, so removing one re-derives the classes — the split happens by
// itself, and congruences that equation had cascaded into disappear with it.
// The dataflow engine supplies the "what depends on this equation" index that
// an e-graph would need parent pointers for (and that ægraphs deliberately
// omit, which is why they cannot be made incremental at all).
//
// Congruence links each node to its form's representative rather than pairing
// congruent nodes with each other: LINEAR in nodes, avoiding the quadratic
// blow-up of materializing the equivalence relation that made hand-rolled
// Datalog equality slow (the cclyzer++ problem egglog describes).
//
// SCOPE. Term ids are numeric here, so this file is congruence closure over a
// FIXED term universe — enough for unification analysis (steensgaard.dl), where
// every term already exists in the source. For rewriting that must CREATE terms,
// saturation.dl uses structural string ids instead, which a rule can construct
// with `concat`.
//
//   dep2 run examples/egraph/congruence.dl \
//     --source 'node=csv:path=node.csv' --source 'eq_input=csv:path=eq.csv'
//
// `-1` is the "no child" sentinel; it is a term, in a class of its own.

.in
.decl node(t: number, op: number, a: number, b: number)
.decl eq_input(x: number, y: number)

.printsize
.decl term(t: number)
.decl eq_edge(x: number, y: number)
.decl cnode(t: number, op: number, la: number, lb: number)

.out
// The class representative of every term. `merge(min)` makes this a FUNCTION
// from term to representative, folded by the lattice join — the piece that
// replaces the union-find, and the piece that can retract.
.decl leader(t: number, rep: number) merge(min)
// The hash-cons. One representative per canonical form, again by lattice fold.
.decl form_rep(op: number, la: number, lb: number, rep: number) merge(min)

.rule
term(T) :- node(T, _, _, _).
term(A) :- node(_, _, A, _).
term(B) :- node(_, _, _, B).
// Asserting an equation introduces its operands as terms (nullary constants).
term(X) :- eq_input(X, _).
term(Y) :- eq_input(_, Y).

eq_edge(X, Y) :- eq_input(X, Y).
eq_edge(Y, X) :- eq_input(X, Y).

// Canonicalize: replace each child with its class representative.
cnode(T, Op, LA, LB) :- node(T, Op, A, B), leader(A, LA), leader(B, LB).

// Hash-cons the canonical forms.
form_rep(Op, LA, LB, T) :- cnode(T, Op, LA, LB).

// Congruence: a node is equal to the representative of its canonical form.
eq_edge(T, R) :- cnode(T, Op, LA, LB), form_rep(Op, LA, LB, R).
eq_edge(R, T) :- cnode(T, Op, LA, LB), form_rep(Op, LA, LB, R).

// The class representative: min label over the equation graph. Recursive, and
// folded in-loop — the fixpoint converges because min is a lattice join.
leader(T, T) :- term(T).
leader(X, L) :- eq_edge(X, Y), leader(Y, L).
// Pointer jumping. Logically redundant — in a monotone reading it derives
// nothing the rule above will not — but it earns its place twice over. Hopping
// through the leader FUNCTION roughly doubles reach per round instead of
// advancing one edge, which takes a long chain from O(N^2) to O(N log N) to
// build and O(N^2) to O(N) to retract. It also decides the one case where this
// recursion has more than one stable state: on a cyclic term table, where a
// congruence can rest on itself, one-hop propagation refuses the merge while
// jumping settles on the answer a destructive union-find gives. See
// `pointer_jumping_selects_the_union_find_fixpoint`.
leader(X, L) :- leader(X, M), leader(M, L).
